      en: "The Wels City Tower is the unmistakable landmark of this Upper Austrian town, standing proudly at the western end of the historic town square. This imposing structure has its roots in the 13th century, originally serving as an integral part of the medieval fortifications and a principal city gate. Its present-day appearance is defined by the magnificent Baroque onion dome added in 1732, which gives the tower its elegant and distinct silhouette. Particularly noteworthy are the ornate coat of arms stones on the facade, including that of Emperor Maximilian I, who maintained a close relationship with Wels. For many years, the tower housed the municipal archives, but today it primarily serves as a popular photographic subject and a symbol of civic pride. The massive construction, featuring wall thicknesses of nearly two meters at the base, bears witness to the tower's former defensive function. The City Tower is not only an architectural gem but also a living witness to the history of Wels, impressively documenting the town's evolution from the Middle Ages to the modern era.",
